Yes, you can absolutely do that! In a free public repo, letting users authenticate with their own Spotify accounts via the official Spotify Web API is the gold standard approach. It keeps your repository free because you don't have to host or pay for music streaming data—Spotify handles all the heavy lifting.
Here is how you can implement the Spotify architecture, alongside a structured blueprint for managing your video "turnout" and "interrupt" clips.
------------------------------
## Part 1: Integrating Spotify (User Authentication)
Since your repo is public and free, never hardcode API keys or host a central server that streams music. Instead, use the Authorization Code Flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange).

[User App] ---> (Logs in via Spotify OAuth) ---> [Receives Access Token]

    |                                                  |
    +-----> [Web Playback SDK] <-----------------------+ (Streams music locally)

## How it works in a public repo:

* Client-Side Auth (PKCE): This allows secure token exchange entirely in the browser. You don't need a secure backend server to hide a "Client Secret," meaning your frontend codebase can remain 100% public.
* Spotify Web Playback SDK: This lets you turn your application into a literal Spotify player directly inside the browser (requires Spotify Premium for full playback control).
* Privacy & Cost: Users log in directly through Spotify's secure pop-up. You never touch their passwords, and your hosting costs stay at exactly zero.

